            <ArticleTitle>Application of Clinical Practice Guidelines for Pain, Agitation, and Delirium.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>241-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cnc.2016.02.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0899-5885(16)30001-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Critically ill patients experience several severe, distressing, and often life-altering symptoms during their intensive care unit stay. A clinical practice guideline released by the American College of Critical Care Medicine provides a template for improving the care and outcomes of the critically ill through evidence-based pain, agitation, and delirium assessment, prevention, and management. Key strategies include the use of valid and reliable assessment tools, setting a desired sedation level target, a focus on light sedation, choosing appropriate sedative medications, the use of nonpharmacologic symptom management strategies, and engaging and empowering patients and their family to play an active role in their intensive care unit care.</AbstractText>
